% Unsatisfiable because a disequality and an equality contradict each other, which
% neither bounds reasoning nor a single branch can see.
%
% x <> y and x = y over 1..2. Nothing propagates at the root: x = y prunes no bound
% (both domains are already 1..2) and a disequality with two unfixed terms says
% nothing. So the refutation needs *both* branches, and each branch is closed by a
% clause rather than by slack: x = 1 forces y = 1 and int_ne then finds the sum at the
% value it must avoid; x = 2 the same. The two nogoods are `x >= 2` and `x < 2`, and
% level 0 resolves them to the empty clause.
%
% That last step is why this model is here rather than a unit test: `rup >= 1 ;` closing
% a refutation that rests on clauses is exactly what D-0022/I-X7 added and what
% Search.rests_on_a_clause chooses, and until int_ne reached the CLI no model could
% reach it.
var 1..2: x :: output_var;
var 1..2: y :: output_var;
constraint int_ne(x, y);
constraint int_eq(x, y);
solve satisfy;
